    <!-- ========================================================
         TRANSFORMATION OF FUNCTIONS — SHIFTS (VIDEO 1)
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="transformations-shifts">
        <title>Transformation of Functions — Shifts (Translations)</title>

        <!-- Video 1: Shifts (Translations) -->
        <p><url href="https://youtu.be/rrvYiHKb2EU">Transformation of Functions — Shifts Video</url></p>

        <p>
            Many functions have graphs that are simple transformations of the parent graphs.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, you can obtain the graph of <m>h(x) = x^2 + 2</m> by shifting the graph of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> upward two units. The shape of the graph does <em effect="bold">not</em> change. The graph is simply moved up.
        </p>

        <p>
            When the shape does not change, the transformation is known as a <term>rigid transformation</term>. In this example we see a <term>vertical translation</term>.
        </p>

        <p>
            In function notation, <m>h</m> and <m>f</m> are related as follows:
        </p>
        <me>h(x) = x^2 + 2 = f(x) + 2</me>

        <ul>
            <li>The <m>+2</m> outside the function changes the <m>y</m>-values, so the entire graph moves <em effect="bold">up 2 units</em>.</li>
            <li>The coefficient of <m>x^2</m> remains <m>1</m>, so the parabola keeps the same width and shape.</li>
        </ul>

        <figure>
            <caption>Vertical Shift: <m>h(x) = x^2 + 2</m></caption>
            <image width="60%">
                <latex-image>
                    <![CDATA[
                    \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.5cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
                        \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-8,-8) grid[step=1] (8,8);
                        \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-8.5,0) -- (8.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
                        \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-8.5) -- (0,8.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
                        \foreach \x in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
                        \foreach \y in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
                        \foreach \x in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
                        \foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
                        \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=-2.8:2.8,samples=100] plot (\x,{\x*\x});
                        \draw[red,thick,domain=-2.6:2.6,samples=100] plot (\x,{\x*\x+2});
                        \fill[red] (0,0) circle (0.12);
                        \fill[blue] (0,2) circle (0.12);
                        \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (4,4.5) {$f(x)=x^2$};
                        \node[red,font=\large] at (3,6.5) {$h(x)=x^2+2$};
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                    \end{adjustbox}
                    ]]>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            Similarly, you can obtain the graph of <m>g(x) = (x - 2)^2</m> by shifting the graph of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> to the right two units.
        </p>

        <p>
            In this case, <m>g(x) = (x - 2)^2 = f(x - 2)</m>.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>The <m>-2</m> inside the input shifts the graph <em effect="bold">right 2 units</em>.</li>
            <li>Horizontal changes occur inside the function and work in the <em>opposite direction</em> of the sign.</li>
        </ul>

        <figure>
            <caption>Horizontal Shift: <m>g(x) = (x-2)^2</m></caption>
            <image width="60%">
                <latex-image>
                    <![CDATA[
                    \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.5cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
                        \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-8,-8) grid[step=1] (8,8);
                        \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-8.5,0) -- (8.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
                        \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-8.5) -- (0,8.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
                        \foreach \x in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
                        \foreach \y in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
                        \foreach \x in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
                        \foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
                        \clip (-8,-8) rectangle (8,8);
                        \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=-2.8:2.8,samples=100] plot (\x,{\x*\x});
                        \draw[red,thick,domain=-2:6,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x-2)^2});
                        \fill[red] (0,0) circle (0.12);
                        \fill[blue] (2,0) circle (0.12);
                        \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (-4,4.5) {$f(x)=x^2$};
                        \node[red,font=\large] at (5,4.5) {$g(x)=(x-2)^2$};
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                    \end{adjustbox}
                    ]]>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <definition>
            <title>Vertical and Horizontal Shifts</title>
            <p>
                Let <m>h</m>, <m>k</m> be positive real numbers. Vertical and horizontal shifts in the graph of <m>y = f(x)</m> are represented as follows.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Vertical shift <m>k</m> units upward: <m>h(x) = f(x) + k</m></li>
                <li>Vertical shift <m>k</m> units downward: <m>h(x) = f(x) - k</m></li>
                <li>Horizontal shift <m>h</m> units to the right: <m>h(x) = f(x - h)</m></li>
                <li>Horizontal shift <m>h</m> units to the left: <m>h(x) = f(x + h)</m></li>
            </ol>
        </definition>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <em>Example.</em> Use the graph of <m>f(x) = x^3</m> to sketch the graph of <m>h(x) = (x + 2)^3 + 1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    Relative to the graph of <m>f(x) = x^3</m>, the graph of <m>h(x) = (x + 2)^3 + 1</m> involves a <em>left shift of two units</em> and an <em>upward shift of one unit</em>.
                </p>

                <!-- FIX 11: h(x)=(x+2)^3+1 extended to full grid -->
                <figure>
                    <caption><m>h(x) = (x+2)^3 + 1</m></caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.5cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
                                \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-8,-8) grid[step=1] (8,8);
                                \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-8.5,0) -- (8.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
                                \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-8.5) -- (0,8.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
                                \foreach \y in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
                                \foreach \x in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
                                \foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
                                \clip (-8,-8) rectangle (8,8);
                                \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=-2.2:2.2,samples=100] plot (\x,{\x*\x*\x});
                                \draw[red,thick,domain=-4.2:3.2,samples=100] plot (\x,{(\x+2)^3+1});
                                \fill[red] (0,0) circle (0.12);
                                \fill[blue] (-2,1) circle (0.12);
                                \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (3.7,3.5) {$f(x)=x^3$};
                                \node[red,font=\large] at (-4.5,5.0) {$h(x)=(x+2)^3+1$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            \end{adjustbox}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ========================================================
         REFLECTIONS
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="transformations-reflections">
        <title>Transformation of Functions — Reflections</title>

        <p>
            The second common type of transformation is a <term>reflection</term>.
        </p>

        <p>
            If you consider the <m>x</m>-axis to be a mirror, the graph of <m>h(x) = -x^2</m> is the mirror image (or reflection) of the graph of <m>f(x) = x^2</m>, reflected across the <m>x</m>-axis.
        </p>

        <!-- FIX 6: Reflection graph drawn across full -8 to 8 grid -->
        <figure>
            <caption>Reflection across the <m>x</m>-axis: <m>h(x) = -x^2</m></caption>
            <image width="60%">
                <latex-image>
                    <![CDATA[
                    \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.5cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
                        \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-8,-8) grid[step=1] (8,8);
                        \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-8.5,0) -- (8.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
                        \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-8.5) -- (0,8.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
                        \foreach \x in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
                        \foreach \y in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
                        \foreach \x in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
                        \foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
                        \clip (-8,-8) rectangle (8,8);
                        \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=-2.8:2.8,samples=100] plot (\x,{\x*\x});
                        \draw[red,thick,domain=-2.8:2.8,samples=100] plot (\x,{-\x*\x});
                        \fill[red] (0,0) circle (0.12);
                        \fill[blue] (0,0) circle (0.12);
                        \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (3.5,3.5) {$f(x)=x^2$};
                        \node[red,font=\large] at (4.5,-3.5) {$h(x)=-x^2$};
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                    \end{adjustbox}
                    ]]>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <definition>
            <title>Reflections in the Coordinate Axes</title>
            <p>
                Reflections in the coordinate axes of the graph of <m>y = f(x)</m> are represented as follows.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Reflection in the <m>x</m>-axis: <m>h(x) = -f(x)</m></li>
                <li>Reflection in the <m>y</m>-axis: <m>h(x) = f(-x)</m></li>
            </ol>
        </definition>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ========================================================
         NON-RIGID TRANSFORMATIONS
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="nonrigid-transformations">
        <title>Non-Rigid Transformations of Functions</title>

        <p>
            Horizontal shifts, vertical shifts, and reflections are <term effect="bold">rigid transformations</term> because the basic shape of the graph is unchanged.
        </p>

        <p>
            These transformations change only the position of the graph in the coordinate plane.
        </p>

        <p>
            <term effect="bold">Nonrigid transformations</term> are those that cause a distortion — a change in the shape of the original graph.
        </p>

        <definition>
            <title>Vertical Stretch and Shrink</title>
            <p>
                Let <m>a</m> be a positive real number. Vertical stretch or shrink in the graph of <m>y = f(x)</m> are represented as follows.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Vertical stretch if <m>a &gt; 1</m>: <m>h(x) = a f(x)</m></li>
                <li>Vertical shrink if <m>0 &lt; a &lt; 1</m>: <m>h(x) = a f(x)</m></li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                <em>Parameter <m>a</m>:</em> Multiplying the function outside changes vertical height; multiplying <m>x</m> inside changes horizontal width. Values <m>a &gt; 1</m> make the graph steeper/narrower, while <m>0 &lt; a &lt; 1</m> makes it flatter/wider.
            </p>
        </definition>

        <definition>
            <title>Horizontal Stretch and Shrink</title>
            <p>
                Let <m>a</m> be a positive real number. Horizontal shrink or stretch in the graph of <m>y = f(x)</m> are represented as follows.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Horizontal shrink if <m>a &gt; 1</m>: <m>h(x) = f(ax)</m></li>
                <li>Horizontal stretch if <m>0 &lt; a &lt; 1</m>: <m>h(x) = f(ax)</m></li>
            </ol>
        </definition>

        <p>
            Compare the graph of <m>f(x) = |x|</m> with the graph of <m>h(x) = 3|x|</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Relative to the graph of <m>f(x) = |x|</m>, the graph of <m>h(x) = 3|x| = 3f(x)</m> is a <term effect="bold">vertical stretch</term> (each <m>y</m>-value is multiplied by <m>3</m>) of the graph of <m>f</m>.
        </p>

        <!-- FIX 9: Vertical stretch graph across full -8 to 8 grid -->
        <figure>
            <caption>Vertical Stretch: <m>h(x) = 3|x|</m></caption>
            <image width="60%">
                <latex-image>
                    <![CDATA[
                   \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.5cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
    \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-8,-8) grid[step=1] (8,8);
    \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-8.5,0) -- (8.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
    \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-8.5) -- (0,8.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
    \foreach \x in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
    \foreach \y in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
    \foreach \x in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
    \foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
    \clip (-8,-8) rectangle (8,8);
    % FIXED: f(x)=|x| now spans the full -8 to 8 domain
    \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=-8:8,samples=100] plot (\x,{abs(\x)});
    % FIXED: h(x)=3|x| also spans the full -8 to 8 domain
    \draw[red,thick,domain=-8:8,samples=100] plot (\x,{3*abs(\x)});
    \fill[red] (1,1) circle (0.12);
    \fill[blue] (1,3) circle (0.12);
    \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (5,3.0) {$f(x)=|x|$};
    \node[red,font=\large] at (2.5,6.5) {$h(x)=3|x|$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{adjustbox}
                    ]]>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            Compare the graph of <m>f(x) = |x|</m> with the graph of <m>g(x) = \frac{1}{3}|x|</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Relative to the graph of <m>f(x) = |x|</m>, the graph of <m>g(x) = \frac{1}{3}|x| = \frac{1}{3}f(x)</m> is a <term effect="bold">vertical shrink</term> (each <m>y</m>-value is multiplied by <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>) of the graph of <m>f</m>.
        </p>

        <!-- FIX 10: Vertical shrink graph across full -8 to 8 grid -->
        <figure>
            <caption>Vertical Shrink: <m>g(x) = \frac{1}{3}|x|</m></caption>
            <image width="60%">
                <latex-image>
                    <![CDATA[
                    \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.5cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
    \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-8,-8) grid[step=1] (8,8);
    \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-8.5,0) -- (8.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
    \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-8.5) -- (0,8.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
    \foreach \x in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
    \foreach \y in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
    \foreach \x in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
    \foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
    \clip (-8,-8) rectangle (8,8);
    % FIXED: f(x)=|x| now spans the full -8 to 8 domain
    \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=-8:8,samples=100] plot (\x,{abs(\x)});
    \draw[red,thick,domain=-8:8,samples=100] plot (\x,{abs(\x)/3});
    \fill[red] (3,3) circle (0.12);
    \fill[blue] (3,1) circle (0.12);
    \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (1.5,4.0) {$f(x)=|x|$};
    \node[red,font=\large] at (3.5,1.8) {$g(x)=\frac13|x|$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{adjustbox}
                    ]]>
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <definition>
            <title>Order of Transformations</title>
            <p>
                If there is more than one transformation, follow this order:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Translate the graph horizontally.</li>
                <li>Stretch or shrink the graph.</li>
                <li>Reflect the graph across an axis.</li>
                <li>Translate the graph vertically.</li>
            </ol>
        </definition>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ========================================================
         EXAMPLE 1: g(x) = sqrt(x) + 4 (Video 2)
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="example-sqrt-plus-4">
        <p><url href="https://youtu.be/7S5HF38DnUY"><m>g(x) = \sqrt{x} + 4</m> Video</url></p>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the function:
                    <me>g(x) = \sqrt{x} + 4</me>
                </p>
                <ol>
                    <li>What is the "Parent Function"?</li>
                    <li>What transformations have been applied?</li>
                    <li>Sketch a graph of the Parent function and the transformed function without plotting points.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <em>1.</em> The parent function is <m>f(x) = \sqrt{x}</m> (square root function).
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>2.</em> The <m>+4</m> is outside the radical, so it shifts the graph <em effect="bold">up 4 units</em>.
                </p>

                <figure>
                    <caption><m>g(x) = \sqrt{x} + 4</m> (shift up by 4)</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.55cm,y=0.55cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
                                \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-2,-2) grid[step=1] (12,10);
                                \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-2.5,0) -- (12.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
                                \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-2.5) -- (0,10.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-2,-1,...,12} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
                                \foreach \y in {-2,-1,...,10} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
                                \foreach \x in {2,4,6,8,10} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
                                \foreach \y in {2,4,6,8} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
                                \node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (0,-0.2) {0};
                                \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=0:12,samples=100] plot (\x,{sqrt(\x)});
                                \draw[red,thick,domain=0:12,samples=100] plot (\x,{sqrt(\x)+4});
                                \fill[red] (0,0) circle (0.12);
                                \fill[blue] (0,4) circle (0.12);
                                \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (7,2.0) {$f(x)=\sqrt{x}$};
                                \node[red,font=\large] at (7,5) {$g(x)=\sqrt{x}+4$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            \end{adjustbox}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ========================================================
         EXAMPLE 2: g(x) = |x+3| - 1 (Video 3)
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="example-abs-plus-3-minus-1">
        <p><url href="https://youtu.be/R9AJGxAJ0QQ"><m>g(x) = |x+3| - 1</m> Video</url></p>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the function:
                    <me>g(x) = |x + 3| - 1</me>
                </p>
                <ol>
                    <li>What is the "Parent Function"?</li>
                    <li>What transformations have been applied?</li>
                    <li>Sketch a graph of the Parent function and the transformed function without plotting points.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <em>1.</em> The parent function is <m>f(x) = |x|</m> (absolute value function).
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>2.</em> The <m>+3</m> inside the absolute value shifts the graph <em effect="bold">left 3 units</em>; the <m>-1</m> outside shifts it <em effect="bold">down 1 unit</em>.
                </p>

                <figure>
                    <caption><m>g(x) = |x+3| - 1</m> (left 3, down 1)</caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.5cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
                                \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-12,-8) grid[step=1] (8,8);
                                \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-12.5,0) -- (8.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
                                \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-8.5) -- (0,8.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-12,-11,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
                                \foreach \y in {-8,-7,...,8} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
                                \foreach \x in {-10,-8,-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
                                \foreach \y in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
                                \clip (-12,-8) rectangle (8,8);
                                \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=-8:8,samples=100] plot (\x,{abs(\x)});
                                \draw[red,thick,domain=-11:8,samples=100] plot (\x,{abs(\x+3)-1});
                                \fill[red] (0,0) circle (0.12);
                                \fill[blue] (-3,-1) circle (0.12);
                                \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (4,.5) {$f(x)=|x|$};
                                \node[red,font=\large] at (-4.5,-2) {$g(x)=|x+3|-1$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            \end{adjustbox}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ========================================================
         EXAMPLE 3: g(x) = -3(x-2)^2 + 1 (Video 4)
         ======================================================== -->

    <subsection xml:id="example-negative-3-squared-plus-1">
        <p><url href="https://youtu.be/QNAYg_ZohrM?t=17"><m>g(x) = -3(x-2)^2 + 1</m> Video</url></p>

        <activity>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the function:
                    <me>g(x) = -3(x - 2)^2 + 1</me>
                </p>
                <ol>
                    <li>What is the "Parent Function"?</li>
                    <li>What transformations have been applied?</li>
                    <li>Sketch a graph of the Parent function and the transformed function without plotting points.</li>
                </ol>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <em>1.</em> The parent function is <m>f(x) = x^2</m> (squaring/quadratic function).
                </p>
                <p>
                    <em>2.</em> The transformations are:
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><m>h = 2</m>: shift <em effect="bold">right 2 units</em></li>
                    <li><m>a = -3</m>: reflect across the <m>x</m>-axis and <em>vertical stretch by 3</em></li>
                    <li><m>k = 1</m>: shift <em effect="bold">up 1 unit</em></li>
                </ul>

                <figure>
                    <caption><m>g(x) = -3(x-2)^2 + 1</m></caption>
                    <image width="60%">
                        <latex-image>
                            <![CDATA[
                            \begin{adjustbox}{max width=\linewidth}
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.5cm,y=0.5cm,>=stealth,line cap=round,line join=round]
                                \draw[gray!50,line width=0.4pt] (-8,-10) grid[step=1] (10,10);
                                \draw[black!80,thick,->] (-8.5,0) -- (10.5,0) node[anchor=west,font=\tiny] {$x$};
                                \draw[black!80,thick,->] (0,-10.5) -- (0,10.5) node[anchor=south,font=\tiny] {$y$};
                                \foreach \x in {-8,-7,...,10} {\draw[black!80,thick] (\x,0.1) -- (\x,-0.1);}
                                \foreach \y in {-10,-9,...,10} {\draw[black!80,thick] (0.1,\y) -- (-0.1,\y);}
                                \foreach \x in {-6,-4,-2,2,4,6,8} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=north] at (\x,-0.2) {$\x$};}
                                \foreach \y in {-8,-6,-4,-2,2,4,6,8} {\node[font=\tiny,anchor=east] at (-0.2,\y) {$\y$};}
                                \clip (-8,-10) rectangle (10,10);
                                \draw[graphblue,thick,domain=-3:3,samples=100] plot (\x,{\x*\x});
                                \draw[red,thick,domain=0:4.2,samples=100] plot (\x,{-3*(\x-2)^2+1});
                                \fill[red] (0,0) circle (0.12);
                                \fill[blue] (2,1) circle (0.12);
                                \node[graphblue,font=\large] at (-4.5,6.0) {$f(x)=x^2$};
                                \node[red,font=\large] at (-4,-5.5) {$g(x)=-3(x-2)^2+1$};
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                            \end{adjustbox}
                            ]]>
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </solution>
        </activity>
    </subsection>

</section>